7
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:34,700
In the previous tutorial we learned about filtration, a technique that allows us to separate

8
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,600
solid and liquid components of a mixture.

9
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:41,560
But what if the components we wish to separate are both in solution?

10
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:45,160
There is nothing about particle size that will allow us to do so.

11
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:49,960
Fortunately, there are separation techniques in chemistry that take advantage of factors

12
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:53,600
other than particle size in order to separate components.

13
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:58,900
These focus on properties such as solubility, polarity, and reactivity.

14
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:03,420
The technique we will go over in this tutorial is called extraction.

15
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:08,040
Extraction is a technique that uses a two-phase system to separate mixtures.

16
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:13,620
The most common form is a liquid-liquid extraction, and in this context, the two-phase system

17
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:17,120
will be an organic phase and an aqueous phase.

18
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:21,780
So in performing this technique, we will separate a desired compound from a mixture

19
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:24,780
by moving it from one solvent to another.

20
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:29,280
To perform this technique, we will be taking the solution and adding a new solvent that

21
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:33,000
dissolves and isolates one compound from the mixture.

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,720
We must use a solvent that is immiscible with the first solvent, so that separate layers

23
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:41,600
will form, as this makes it quite easy to separate them.

24
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:44,340
To do this, we'll need a separatory funnel.

25
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:49,540
This is a tool that is used to separate two immiscible liquids of different densities.

26
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:54,760
Once again, in most cases, the two liquids will be an organic solvent, plus whatever compounds

27
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:59,780
are dissolved in it, and then water, plus whatever compounds are dissolved in it.

28
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:04,340
It is usually the case that the aqueous layer has a greater density than the organic layer,

29
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:08,660
so it goes to the bottom while the organic solvent stays on top, as gravity will pull

30
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:11,580
the denser layer down with greater force.

31
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:16,180
However, some organic solvents are more dense than water and will thus occupy the

32
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:21,180
bottom layer, so it is important to consult a table of densities to be sure as to

33
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,060
which layer is which.

34
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:27,120
When in doubt, if you want to identify which layer is which, add a bit of water.

35
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,880
Whichever layer grows, that's the aqueous layer.

36
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:34,720
Now let's get more familiar with the intricacies of the separatory funnel.

37
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:40,120
As we can see here, the funnel has a stopper at the top, and a stopcock at the bottom.

38
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,600
This middle region is where our separation will take place.

39
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:47,240
To get this ready, you might have to add a little bit of grease to the stopper and

40
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:50,280
or stopcock, since they tend to get stuck.

41
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:54,620
To set up for the extraction, we'll start with a ring stand attached to a support like

42
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:55,620
this.

43
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:00,100
We'll then place our separatory funnel in the ring and make sure everything is secure.

44
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,620
To verify that the stopcock works, add some water.

45
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:07,100
Make sure there are no leaks when the valve is closed.

46
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:09,660
Open the valve to see how the water flows.

47
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:11,860
Once satisfied, remove the water.

48
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:14,340
We are now ready to get started.

49
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:18,600
So let's say this is a reaction mixture that we just got from performing a chemical

50
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,480
reaction in some organic solvent.

51
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:25,840
Most of the time you'll need to quench a reaction once it's finished by adding water.

52
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:30,900
This means to stop a reaction mixture from reacting further, often with dilute weak acid

53
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:34,480
or base, to neutralize any ionic species.

54
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:38,680
This means that after any chemical reaction, you will need to perform an extraction to

55
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:43,360
separate your organic compounds from whatever is in the aqueous layer.

56
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:47,960
In this particular case, rather than a reaction mixture, we simply have a mixture containing

57
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:54,680
benzoic acid and 2-methoxy naphthalene dissolved in diethyl ether, and our goal is to obtain

58
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:58,560
each of these two compounds in a pure form.

59
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:02,840
These are both soluble in diethyl ether, but insoluble in water.

60
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:08,720
However, if we add aqueous base to this mixture, benzoic acid will deprotonate, becoming

61
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:14,240
the benzoate anion, which has a formal negative charge, and will thus become water soluble

62
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:16,880
and move into the aqueous phase.

63
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:21,560
Since 2-methoxy naphthalene will not react, that will remain in the organic phase, and

64
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,700
the two compounds will have separated.

65
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:28,560
With our strategy understood, we can begin performing the technique.

66
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:33,520
Making sure that the stopcock is closed, let's transfer our mixture to the separatory

67
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:36,600
funnel using a conical glass funnel.

68
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:40,460
This is our organic layer.

69
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:44,340
To make sure we have transferred everything, rinse the beaker that held the mixture with

70
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:46,940
ether and transfer that as well.

71
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:51,060
Had this been the product of a chemical reaction, it would already have been quenched and thus

72
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:53,900
have formed organic and aqueous layers.

73
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:58,260
But since we are just doing an isolated extraction, let's go ahead and perform the necessary

74
00:04:58,260 --> 00:05:01,780
acid base reaction right in the separatory funnel.

75
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:07,480
To separate benzoic acid from the mixture, we will add 30 milliliters of 1 molar sodium

76
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,240
hydroxide to the separatory funnel.

77
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:15,940
This will pass through the organic layer, react with benzoic acid on the way to form

78
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:22,580
sodium benzoate, and drag that down to the newly forming aqueous layer on the bottom.

79
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:27,660
To make sure the reaction goes to completion, we need to mix the contents of the funnel.

80
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:32,820
We will do this by inverting it and lightly swirling a few times, holding the stopper down

81
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:37,220
with one finger as we do so to ensure that nothing spills out.

82
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:39,700
Mix for about 10 seconds each time.

83
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:44,900
After each round of mixing, hold the funnel upside down and open the stopcock like this

84
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:49,140
to vent any gases that accumulate from the reaction.

85
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:51,460
Close it, and then continue mixing.

86
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:55,340
When gases are vented, you will hear a brief hissing sound.

87
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:59,980
When the sound no longer occurs, no more gas is being produced, and we can be confident

88
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:02,260
that mixing is complete.

89
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:04,900
After mixing, it is time to separate.

90
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:09,420
Return your separatory funnel to the ring support and allow the contents to settle.

91
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:12,860
You should see the two layers separate clearly.

92
00:06:12,860 --> 00:06:18,340
You can now open the stopcock to let the bottom layer drop into a clean flask.

93
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:20,220
This is where you have to be careful.

94
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:25,380
Watch the aqueous layer drip slowly so that you can stop it right before any of the top

95
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:27,020
layer goes through.

96
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:31,500
When the line separating the layers reaches the stopcock, close it.

97
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:34,020
You have now separated the two layers.

98
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:37,940
The top layer can be removed by pouring it through the top of the funnel into a

99
00:06:37,940 --> 00:06:39,780
separate flask.

100
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:44,560
Be sure to pour it out the top and not through the stopcock, as pouring out the bottom would

101
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:46,360
contaminate it.

102
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:50,780
With most extractions, we will need to do more than one separation so as to maximize

103
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:55,740
your yield, as not all of the benzoic acid made it out of the organic layer.

104
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:59,540
So let's put this first aqueous extract to the side.

105
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:05,180
Return the organic layer to the separatory funnel, and make sure everything is secured.

106
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:11,420
We can then add more aqueous base to the organic layer, and mix just as we did before.

107
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:15,860
Allow the layers to separate, and we can now collect the bottom layer again, adding it

108
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:21,990
to the original extract.

109
00:07:21,990 --> 00:07:24,990
So now the extraction itself is complete.

110
00:07:24,990 --> 00:07:30,030
We have some aqueous sodium benzoate, and then we have two methoxy naphthalene still

111
00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:31,550
in the organic solvent.

112
00:07:31,550 --> 00:07:33,790
Let's isolate this first.

113
00:07:33,790 --> 00:07:39,070
The organic layer will likely contain residual water from the multiple washes that we performed.

114
00:07:39,590 --> 00:07:41,130
How do we get it out?

115
00:07:41,130 --> 00:07:43,390
We use a drying agent.

116
00:07:43,390 --> 00:07:50,150
A drying agent is a compound like sodium sulfate that removes water when added to a solution.

117
00:07:50,150 --> 00:07:55,390
The water should bind to the drying agent, and thus join the solid phase.

118
00:07:55,390 --> 00:08:00,110
Keep adding the drying agent until you see a clear solution with all of the solid clumped

119
00:08:00,110 --> 00:08:01,270
at the bottom.

120
00:08:01,270 --> 00:08:05,870
You will know it's dry when lightly shaking the flask does not cause any solid to move

121
00:08:05,870 --> 00:08:07,710
around the solution.

122
00:08:07,710 --> 00:08:12,350
To separate the drying agent from the liquid, we'll need to do a gravity filtration.

123
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:16,370
Since we discussed this at great length in the previous tutorial, we won't go over

124
00:08:16,370 --> 00:08:22,150
the details here, but set up your filtration, and pour your dried organic layer in.

125
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:26,510
The drying agent will remain on the filter paper, and the target molecule will move

126
00:08:26,510 --> 00:08:28,710
through with the solvent.

127
00:08:28,710 --> 00:08:33,310
Make sure to rinse the flask with more solvent so that everything is transferred.

128
00:08:33,310 --> 00:08:38,050
Then just evaporate the solvent on a hot plate, as ether is rather volatile, and we will be

129
00:08:38,050 --> 00:08:41,870
left with a residue of pure 2-methoxy naphthalene.

130
00:08:41,870 --> 00:08:46,710
The aqueous layer will be a bit trickier, as we wanted to isolate benzoic acid, but

131
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:49,030
here we have sodium benzoate.

132
00:08:49,030 --> 00:08:53,710
To get benzoic acid, we will need to do another acid-base reaction to protonate the

133
00:08:53,710 --> 00:08:54,710
benzoate.

134
00:08:54,710 --> 00:08:59,210
Let's add about 5 mL of 6-molar hydrochloric acid.

135
00:08:59,210 --> 00:09:03,550
You may need to add a little more than this, just monitor the solution with litmus paper

136
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:06,810
and add it until the solution starts to turn acidic.

137
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:12,890
A pH of around 2 means that the acid is no longer neutralizing base, but is turning the

138
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:18,530
solution acidic, which means the acid is in excess and the reaction is complete.

139
00:09:18,530 --> 00:09:21,890
As benzoic acid forms, it will begin to precipitate.

140
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:26,990
If this is happening very slowly, the flask can be placed in an ice bath or cold water

141
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:30,050
bath to encourage solid formation.

142
00:09:30,050 --> 00:09:35,310
Once precipitation of benzoic acid is complete, it can be collected by vacuum filtration.

143
00:09:35,310 --> 00:09:39,750
Again, we have described this technique in the previous tutorial, so we will not go

144
00:09:39,750 --> 00:09:44,850
into detail here, but collect your solid on the Büchner funnel and allow it to dry

145
00:09:44,850 --> 00:09:52,280
on the bench for some time, then moving it to an oven if further drying is necessary.

146
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:56,060
And with that, we have completed a liquid-liquid extraction.

147
00:09:56,060 --> 00:10:01,720
We had two organic molecules mixed together in an organic solvent, and we separated them

148
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:07,480
based on their chemical properties, namely their propensity to undergo an acid-base reaction.

149
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:11,540
This is an important tool that is used constantly in organic chemistry.

150
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:16,700
It is also widely used in industry for the extraction of natural products, ranging from

151
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:21,580
caffeine extraction from leaves to metal extraction from minerals.

152
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:25,580
With this now understood, let's move forward and check out some other techniques.
